@* The controls on one row of a list: the common ones, then a ⋯ for the rest.

   A row is scanned, not read. Six icons on every row of a table is six times as much to look past
   as one, and five of them are things nobody does from a list — renaming a container, copying its
   id, pushing an image. Those are not removed, they are put where they cost nothing until wanted.

   Deliberately not ActionRow's measuring: there is one of these per row, and a resize observer per
   row is four hundred of them on the containers page. How many fit is also the wrong question here
   — the answer is a fixed small number regardless of how wide the window gets. *@

<div class="rowacts">
    @foreach (var action in Inline)
    {
        var captured = action;

        <Button Label="@action.Label"
                Icon="action.Icon"
                IconOnly="true"
                Tooltip="@action.Tooltip"
                Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost"
                Danger="action.Danger"
                Disabled="action.Disabled"
                OnClick="() => OnAction.InvokeAsync(captured.Id)" />
    }

    <OverflowMenu Actions="Rest" Label="@MenuLabel" TriggerClass="btn--ghost" OnAction="OnAction" />
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The actions, most common first. The order decides what stays on the row.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public IReadOnlyList<RowAction> Actions { get; set; } = [];

    [Parameter] public EventCallback<string> OnAction { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// How many stay on the row. Two is the usual answer — the thing this row's state invites you
    /// to do, and the one next to it.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public int Visible { get; set; } = 2;

    [Parameter] public string MenuLabel { get; set; } = "More actions";

    /// <summary>
    /// How many actually stay on the row.
    /// <para>
    /// One more than asked for when that would empty the menu: a ⋯ holding a single item is worse
    /// than the button it replaced — same width, one more click.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    int Shown => Actions.Count == Visible + 1 ? Actions.Count : Visible;

    IReadOnlyList<RowAction> Inline => [.. Actions.Take(Shown)];

    IReadOnlyList<RowAction> Rest => [.. Actions.Skip(Shown)];
}
